          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"zuccoli-lamore-di-loredana-07cd774bb417707e\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":2,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.91,\"analysis\":\"Berto’s “Vi amo teneramente” primarily avows as true his continuing tender love for Loredana. Its placement within an outburst introduced by “di repente proruppe” gives independent, moderate support to expressive/reflexive force: his joy and newly unrestrained desire appear to produce a sudden verbal discharge. The words themselves do not clearly undertake a commitment; his nearby offer to devote his life to her is distinct contextual material.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The degree of E is uncertain: “proruppe” positively frames the speech as an affective outburst, but the love clause remains coherent as a deliberate avowal and reassurance. The surrounding courtship creates slight pragmatic pressure toward P, though no undertaking is encoded in the highlighted clause itself.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Berto directly addresses Loredana with the present-tense avowal “Vi amo teneramente” (“I love you tenderly”).\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Berto speaks after Loredana has fled Filippo and sought refuge with him. Elated by her presence and openly desirous of replacing Filippo, he presents his love as deep, continuing, and evidence that he is no longer merely thoughtless or capricious. The declaration is therefore both an earnest-seeming avowal and part of an interested courtship attempt; Loredana immediately rejects reciprocity. His motives and pressure on her complicate the scene but do not alter the core T/E analysis.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Vi amo teneramente e spero di potervi rassicurare” (“I love you tenderly and hope that I may reassure you”).\",\"supports\":\"The explicit present-tense clause strongly supports truth-conditional avowal; its coordination with hoped-for reassurance also shows purposeful communication.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The speech is introduced by “E di repente proruppe” (“And suddenly he burst out”), after Berto is described as “ebbro di gioia” and possessed by “un desiderio non più rattenuto.”\",\"supports\":\"Provides positive, though not conclusive, evidence that the speech partly functions as an affect-driven expressive discharge, supporting E independently of mere intensity.\",\"confidence\":0.9},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Earlier Berto says that Loredana mistook his feeling for a caprice, “mentre io l'ho amata e l'amo con profondo sentimento.”\",\"supports\":\"Frames the highlighted declaration as a repeated claim about a continuing deep emotional state, strengthening T.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand Berto says, “io sono libero e solo, posso dedicarvi intera la mia vita,” while Loredana answers after the highlighted utterance, “Non vi amerò mai.”\",\"supports\":\"Establishes the courtship setting and Loredana’s interpretation as a bid for reciprocal love, while keeping the explicit life-devotion proposal distinct from the core love avowal; hence little independent P support for the highlighted words.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is tagged as affirmative present tense, main-or-embedded syntax, and a valid explicit first-person love expression.\",\"supports\":\"Corroborates identification of the core event as a direct present-tense love utterance.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the avowal, while E captures the textually marked affective outburst. The surrounding courtship, desire, and attempted reassurance are contextual features rather than evidence of a missing core-force category.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
